  "glossary": {
    "pearson": "Pearson r measures how two series move together across markets, from −1 to +1.\n+1 = perfect lockstep\n0 = no linear link\n−1 = perfect opposite\n\nHere each point is ONE distributor (not one dealer).\nSo this is a cross-market prior — NOT a correlation inside IBX or IUK.",
    "section_d": "IDS Section D = Parts standards in the dealer self-evaluation (stock, counter, genuine display, targets, fill tracking, etc.).\nShown as % of maximum points for that section.\nD avg = average across scored dealers in the country.\nIt is a PROCESS / STANDARDS score — not € sales.",
    "d_min": "Lowest Section D % among scored dealers in that country.\nA big gap between D avg and D min = 'floor problem': the network average looks fine but a few dealers are very weak.",
    "genuine_mix": "Genuine parts share (€ mix) = Genuine € ÷ (Genuine € + BVP €).\n\nYES — it is how high genuine is compared with BVP inside that basket.\nExample: 80% means €80 genuine and €20 BVP out of €100 in that mix.\n\nIt is NOT 'genuine vs all independent aftermarket'.\nIt is NOT PMS capture %.\nHigh mix (e.g. 85–98%) = mostly genuine in the Genuine+BVP euro mix.\nLow mix (e.g. <45–50%) = BVP takes a large € share of that basket.\n\nIsrael/UMI can show a very low mix — that is a mix/policy story, not fill.",
    "pms_total": "PMS total capture = actual parts sales ÷ expected wallet (all PMS lines).\nPRIMARY commercial lens — sales is sales (includes BVP/total basket).\nHigh IDS D does NOT automatically mean high PMS capture (in this study the cross-market link is even slightly negative).",
    "pms_genuine": "PMS genuine capture = same idea as total capture, but only the genuine line type.\nExtra lens — do not replace total capture with this as the headline.",
    "fill": "KPI first fill rate = share of parts demand lines shipped from stock on first request (KPI Master).\nHigher is usually better ops (fewer backorders).\nRough guide: well above ~90% looks strong; ~60% (e.g. Petros) is soft.\nAlways read with MOS and park age — older shrinking parks often need more odd/slow-moving parts, which can drag fill even with good processes.",
    "mos": "MOS = Months Of Supply (days in KPI Master, averaged).\nRoughly: how many days of stock you are carrying vs sales pace.\n\nWhat is 'high' MOS?\n• Lean: often ~40–70 days (e.g. IBX ~47 in this extract)\n• Typical/elevated: ~90–120 days\n• High: ~140+ days (capital tied in stock; e.g. UMI ~147)\n• Extreme: 200–300+ days (e.g. some MIDI readings) — investigate\n\nHigh MOS is not 'good' by itself. Too high = overstock / slow movers. Too low with poor fill = stockouts. Target bands are market-specific.",
    "gps_spu": "GPS €/UIO (PRISM) = parts € intensity per Unit In Operation (parc vehicle).\n\nIMPORTANT — warranty: the headline figure used here is spend_per_unit_eur, which INCLUDES warranty parts (warranty is embedded in ERP genuine sales).\nAn ex-warranty €/UIO also exists and is shown on cards as '€/UIO ex-w'.\n\nThis is the better-positioned '€ per unit' metric vs sparse KPI Parts Sales per Unit.\nDifferent from genuine mix % and from IDS Section D.\nTraffic light (green/amber) is on the INCLUSIVE €/UIO unless labelled ex-w.",
    "gps_spu_ex": "GPS €/UIO excluding warranty parts.\nHeadline GPS on cards still includes warranty; use this to see how much warranty inflates €/UIO (large gaps e.g. on UMI).",
    "gps_light": "PRISM traffic light on GPS €/UIO intensity for that distributor (on the INCLUSIVE / with-warranty figure unless labelled ex-w).\nGreen = intensity in a healthier band vs peers/targets; amber = soft. Not an IDS result.",
    "park_age": "Park ageing proxy from UIO.xlsx stock time series (not a true mean vehicle age).\n5y net park change % = (UIO_2024 − UIO_2019) / UIO_2024.\nNegative = shrinking parc → remaining fleet tends to be older (harder repairs, more odd parts, lower first-fill risk).\nStrongly positive = younger / expanding parc.",
    "kpi_spu": "KPI Master row: Parts Sales per Unit in € (€ parts ÷ unit base).\nSame FAMILY of idea as GPS €/UIO (intensity per vehicle), but sparsely filled.\nWhen blank: prefer GPS €/UIO — it is better positioned for this comparison.\nSee §05. The ~0.98 correlation uses just 3 markets — ignore that bar.",
    "cap_d_only": "COUNT of dealers in this country tagged 'D-only' for Kaizen.\n\nMeaning: weak on IDS Parts (Section D), but Service (Section C) is OK.\nExample: CAP D-only = 4 → four dealers need a Parts/counter CAP, not a workshop CAP.\n\nThis is NOT question D.1 or D.4 from the Excel — it is a headcount.",
    "cap_d_plus_c": "COUNT of dealers in this country tagged 'D+C' for Kaizen.\n\nMeaning: weak on BOTH Parts (D) and Service (C).\nExample: CAP D+C = 1 → one dealer needs a combined workshop + parts CAP (RO attach / pull-through), not only a counter fix.\n\nThis is NOT an IDS question number — it is a headcount.",
    "cap_c_only": "COUNT of dealers weak on Service (C) only, while Parts (D) looks OK.\nAgain a headcount — not an Excel question code.",
    "benelux": "Benelux result-report tab = BE + NL dealers combined.\nIf we also keep BE and NL tabs, summing countries double-counts those dealers.\nHygiene drops the Benelux rollup from network averages.",
    "zero_shells": "Dealers with 0% / blank totals — often empty MIDI self-eval shells still in the file.\nThey pull averages down unfairly, so hygiene excludes them from scored stats.",
    "raw_rows": "Every dealer row in the dashboard extract before hygiene filters.",
    "clean_rows": "Dealer rows after dropping Benelux rollup and 0% unscored shells."
  },
